#: VirtualAtoms.md:1
msgid "Dealing with virtual atoms"
msgstr ""

#: VirtualAtoms.md:3
msgid ""
"Virtual atoms are used to refer to the positions used in calculating CVs but"
" where no atoms can be found. The most commonly used example of a virtual "
"atom is a centre of mass."
msgstr ""

#: VirtualAtoms.md:6
msgid ""
"In older versions of PLUMED, these virtual atom positions were passed back "
"to the Atoms class. Consequently, if the MD code passed $n$ atoms to PLUMED,"
" and PLUMED went on to define $k$ virtual atom positions; you could choose "
"from amongst $n+k$ positions when deciding how to calculate your CVs."
msgstr ""

#: VirtualAtoms.md:10
msgid ""
"You can do the same in newer versions of PLUMED. Now, however, the virtual "
"atom positions are stored in three PLMD::Value objects so the passing is "
"consistent with the ideas discussed [here](Passing.md). To make this "
"possible PLMD::ActionWithValue contains the following code in its "
"constructor:"
msgstr ""

#: VirtualAtoms.md:38
msgid ""
"This code ensures that every Action that inherits from ActionAtomistic has "
"five std::vector objects of pointers to PLMD::Value objects called `xpos`, "
"`ypos`, `zpos`, `masv` and `chargev`. These std::vector objects contain "
"pointers to the PLMD::Value objects that contain information about the atoms"
" passed from the MD code and the positions of the virtual atoms."
msgstr ""

#: VirtualAtoms.md:42
msgid ""
"PLUMED works out the dependencies for this new ActionAtomistic object in "
"`requestAtoms` by using the following code:"
msgstr ""

#: VirtualAtoms.md:66
msgid ""
"The advantage of this new arrangement over the old one is that the code for "
"applying forces on virtual atom positions is the same as the code for "
"applying forces on CVs."
msgstr ""
